1 REM ============================================================
2 REM  t58_virtual_terminal.bpp
3 REM  BASIC++ Virtual Terminal & Console Test & Tutorial
4 REM ============================================================
5 REM
6 REM  BASIC++ provides terminal I/O through ANSI escape sequences
7 REM  (Windows 11, FreeDOS) and ncurses (Linux). The virtual
8 REM  terminal layer abstracts these differences so programs
9 REM  can control the screen portably.
10 REM
11 REM  KEY COMMANDS FOR TERMINAL CONTROL:
12 REM    CLS          - Clear the entire screen
13 REM    HOME         - Move cursor to row 1, column 1
14 REM    LOCATE r,c   - Move cursor to row r, column c
15 REM    COLOR n      - Set text foreground color (0-15)
16 REM    WIDTH n      - Set screen width (40/80 columns)
17 REM    CSRLIN       - Returns current cursor row
18 REM    POS(0)       - Returns current cursor column
19 REM
20 REM  PRINT CONTROLS:
21 REM    PRINT TAB(n)   - Move to column n before printing
22 REM    PRINT SPC(n)   - Print n spaces
23 REM    ; (semicolon)  - No newline, next item immediately follows
24 REM    , (comma)      - Tab to next print zone (14 chars wide)
25 REM
26 DIALECT "GWBS"
27 REM
100 REM === CLS - Clear Screen ===
101 REM CLS clears the entire screen and moves the cursor
102 REM to the top-left corner (home position).
103 REM On ANSI terminals: sends ESC[2J ESC[H
110 TEST "CLS"
120 CLS
130 REM Screen cleared, no crash
140 ENDTEST
200 REM === HOME ===
201 REM HOME moves the cursor to row 1, column 1 without
202 REM clearing the screen. Equivalent to LOCATE 1,1.
210 TEST "HOME"
220 HOME
230 REM Cursor at top-left
240 ENDTEST
300 REM === LOCATE row, col ===
301 REM LOCATE positions the cursor at a specific row and column.
302 REM Row 1 is the top line, Column 1 is the leftmost position.
303 REM After LOCATE, the next PRINT appears at that position.
310 TEST "LOCATE"
320 LOCATE 5,10
330 REM Cursor now at row 5, column 10
340 LOCATE 1,1
350 REM Cursor back to top-left
360 ENDTEST
400 REM === CSRLIN and POS(0) ===
401 REM CSRLIN returns the current row of the cursor (1-based).
402 REM POS(0) returns the current column of the cursor (1-based).
403 REM These are useful for saving/restoring cursor position.
410 TEST "CSRLIN and POS"
420 LET R=CSRLIN
430 LET C=POS(0)
440 ASSERT R>=0
450 ASSERT C>=0
460 ENDTEST
500 REM === COLOR ===
501 REM COLOR sets the text foreground color.
502 REM Standard colors (vary by dialect):
503 REM   0=Black   1=Blue     2=Green   3=Cyan
504 REM   4=Red     5=Magenta  6=Brown   7=White
505 REM   8-15 = Bright versions of 0-7
506 REM
507 REM In GW-BASIC mode: COLOR foreground, background
508 REM In Sinclair mode: INK, PAPER, BORDER
510 TEST "COLOR"
520 COLOR 7
530 REM White text
540 COLOR 0
550 REM Black text (reset to default)
560 ENDTEST
600 REM === WIDTH ===
601 REM WIDTH n sets the logical screen width.
602 REM Common values: 40 (narrow), 80 (standard).
603 REM This affects TAB positioning and word-wrapping.
610 TEST "WIDTH"
620 WIDTH 80
630 REM Standard 80-column mode
640 ENDTEST
700 REM === TAB and SPC ===
701 REM TAB(n) moves the cursor to column n before printing.
702 REM SPC(n) outputs n space characters.
703 REM These are used inside PRINT statements for formatting.
710 TEST "TAB and SPC"
720 PRINT TAB(10);"TABBED"
730 PRINT SPC(5);"SPACED"
740 REM TAB moved to column 10, SPC inserted 5 spaces
750 ENDTEST
800 REM === PRINT zones ===
801 REM Comma in PRINT advances to the next "zone" (default 14 chars).
802 REM Semicolon suppresses the newline, next item follows immediately.
803 REM No separator at end of PRINT means newline is printed.
810 TEST "PRINT formatting"
820 PRINT "A";"B";"C"
830 REM Prints: ABC (no spaces between items)
840 PRINT "A","B","C"
850 REM Prints: A             B             C (tabbed zones)
860 PRINT
870 REM Prints a blank line
880 ENDTEST
900 REM === Sinclair terminal attributes ===
901 REM In Sinclair dialect, screen attributes use:
902 REM   BORDER n   - Set border color (0-7)
903 REM   PAPER n    - Set background color (0-7)
904 REM   INK n      - Set foreground color (0-7)
905 REM   BRIGHT n   - 0=normal, 1=bright
906 REM   FLASH n    - 0=steady, 1=flashing
907 REM   INVERSE n  - 0=normal, 1=inverse video
908 REM   OVER n     - 0=normal, 1=overprint (XOR)
910 DIALECT "SINC"
920 TEST "Sinclair attrs"
930 BORDER 1
940 PAPER 7
950 INK 0
960 BRIGHT 1
970 FLASH 0
980 INVERSE 0
990 OVER 0
995 ENDTEST
9999 END
